            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-9547",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "curl: curl: Man-in-the-middle attack via SSH host key bypass"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When a libcurl-based application performs transfers via `SCP://` or `SFTP://`\nand utilizes the `CURLOPT_SSH_KEYFUNCTION` callback, it may silently accept an\nuntrusted server. This vulnerability occurs when a server presents a host key\ntype that does not match the specific key type already recorded for that host\nin the `known_hosts` file. Instead of rejecting the mismatch, the callback\nmechanism fails to properly enforce the restriction, allowing the connection\nto succeed without warning and risking a potential man-in-the-middle attack."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-9547",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-9547\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: libcurl4t64\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-9547](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-9547)\nWhen a libcurl-based application performs transfers via `SCP://` or `SFTP://`\nand utilizes the `CURLOPT_SSH_KEYFUNCTION` callback, it may silently accept an\nuntrusted server. This vulnerability occurs when a server presents a host key\ntype that does not match the specific key type already recorded for that host\nin the `known_hosts` file. Instead of rejecting the mismatch, the callback\nmechanism fails to properly enforce the restriction, allowing the connection\nto succeed without warning and risking a potential man-in-the-middle attack.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-9547**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|libcurl4t64||[CVE-2026-9547](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-9547)|\n\nWhen a libcurl-based application performs transfers via `SCP://` or `SFTP://`\nand utilizes the `CURLOPT_SSH_KEYFUNCTION` callback, it may silently accept an\nuntrusted server. This vulnerability occurs when a server presents a host key\ntype that does not match the specific key type already recorded for that host\nin the `known_hosts` file. Instead of rejecting the mismatch, the callback\nmechanism fails to properly enforce the restriction, allowing the connection\nto succeed without warning and risking a potential man-in-the-middle attack."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-53910",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "diffutils: heap‑based buffer overflow due to multiple signed integer overflows in line‑mapping calculations"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "diff3 tool from GNU diffutils is vulnerable to a heap‑based buffer overflow due to multiple signed integer overflows in line‑mapping calculations. Incorrect arithmetic in mapping line ranges can result in corrupted values being used for memory allocation and loop bounds.\nWhen processing crafted diff output, these overflows may cause the application to allocate insufficient memory and subsequently perform out‑of‑bounds writes during internal processing. \nAn attacker who can control the output of the diff program used by diff3 (e.g. via --diff-program pointing to a malicious script) can trigger out-of-bounds writes, resulting in a crash and potentially remote code execution depending on the environment.\n\n\nThis issue has been fixed in commit 9ff04d5b84743e331e80b589335a52c5480d1815 \n\nNOTE:\nThe project maintainers claim that this is not a security issue. They state that the worst outcome this issue can cause is a crash of diff and that it cannot be used to escalate privileges."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-53910",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-53910\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: diffutils\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-53910](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-53910)\ndiff3 tool from GNU diffutils is vulnerable to a heap‑based buffer overflow due to multiple signed integer overflows in line‑mapping calculations. Incorrect arithmetic in mapping line ranges can result in corrupted values being used for memory allocation and loop bounds.\nWhen processing crafted diff output, these overflows may cause the application to allocate insufficient memory and subsequently perform out‑of‑bounds writes during internal processing. \nAn attacker who can control the output of the diff program used by diff3 (e.g. via --diff-program pointing to a malicious script) can trigger out-of-bounds writes, resulting in a crash and potentially remote code execution depending on the environment.\n\n\nThis issue has been fixed in commit 9ff04d5b84743e331e80b589335a52c5480d1815 \n\nNOTE:\nThe project maintainers claim that this is not a security issue. They state that the worst outcome this issue can cause is a crash of diff and that it cannot be used to escalate privileges.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-53910**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|diffutils||[CVE-2026-53910](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-53910)|\n\ndiff3 tool from GNU diffutils is vulnerable to a heap‑based buffer overflow due to multiple signed integer overflows in line‑mapping calculations. Incorrect arithmetic in mapping line ranges can result in corrupted values being used for memory allocation and loop bounds.\nWhen processing crafted diff output, these overflows may cause the application to allocate insufficient memory and subsequently perform out‑of‑bounds writes during internal processing. \nAn attacker who can control the output of the diff program used by diff3 (e.g. via --diff-program pointing to a malicious script) can trigger out-of-bounds writes, resulting in a crash and potentially remote code execution depending on the environment.\n\n\nThis issue has been fixed in commit 9ff04d5b84743e331e80b589335a52c5480d1815 \n\nNOTE:\nThe project maintainers claim that this is not a security issue. They state that the worst outcome this issue can cause is a crash of diff and that it cannot be used to escalate privileges."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
