            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-58050",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "libssh2: libssh2: Heap buffer overflow via integer overflow in publickey attribute allocation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "libssh2 through 1.11.1 reads an attacker-controlled 32-bit attribute count from a publickey-subsystem response and uses it in the allocation num_attrs * sizeof(libssh2_publickey_attribute) without bounds checking, so on 32-bit platforms the multiplication overflows to an undersized buffer. A malicious SSH server can then drive the attribute-parsing loop to write past the allocation, causing a heap buffer overflow in a connecting libssh2 client."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-58050",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-58050\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: libssh2-1t64\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-58050](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-58050)\nlibssh2 through 1.11.1 reads an attacker-controlled 32-bit attribute count from a publickey-subsystem response and uses it in the allocation num_attrs * sizeof(libssh2_publickey_attribute) without bounds checking, so on 32-bit platforms the multiplication overflows to an undersized buffer. A malicious SSH server can then drive the attribute-parsing loop to write past the allocation, causing a heap buffer overflow in a connecting libssh2 client.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-58050**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|libssh2-1t64||[CVE-2026-58050](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-58050)|\n\nlibssh2 through 1.11.1 reads an attacker-controlled 32-bit attribute count from a publickey-subsystem response and uses it in the allocation num_attrs * sizeof(libssh2_publickey_attribute) without bounds checking, so on 32-bit platforms the multiplication overflows to an undersized buffer. A malicious SSH server can then drive the attribute-parsing loop to write past the allocation, causing a heap buffer overflow in a connecting libssh2 client."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 7.5,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-58051",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "libssh2: libssh2: Denial of service or information disclosure via malformed SSH publickey response"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "libssh2 through 1.11.1 grows its publickey list with SSH2_REALLOC but does not zero-initialize new entries before parsing populates them, so a parse failure reaching the cleanup path leaves libssh2_publickey_list_free operating on an uninitialized entry. A malicious SSH server offering the publickey subsystem can use a malformed response to make cleanup free an uninitialized, attacker-influenceable attrs pointer in a connecting libssh2 client."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-58051",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-58051\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: libssh2-1t64\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-58051](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-58051)\nlibssh2 through 1.11.1 grows its publickey list with SSH2_REALLOC but does not zero-initialize new entries before parsing populates them, so a parse failure reaching the cleanup path leaves libssh2_publickey_list_free operating on an uninitialized entry. A malicious SSH server offering the publickey subsystem can use a malformed response to make cleanup free an uninitialized, attacker-influenceable attrs pointer in a connecting libssh2 client.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-58051**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|libssh2-1t64||[CVE-2026-58051](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-58051)|\n\nlibssh2 through 1.11.1 grows its publickey list with SSH2_REALLOC but does not zero-initialize new entries before parsing populates them, so a parse failure reaching the cleanup path leaves libssh2_publickey_list_free operating on an uninitialized entry. A malicious SSH server offering the publickey subsystem can use a malformed response to make cleanup free an uninitialized, attacker-influenceable attrs pointer in a connecting libssh2 client."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
