            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-68368",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: Kernel: Denial of Service in md driver via uninitialized bioset"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmd: init bioset in mddev_init\n\nIO operations may be needed before md_run(), such as updating metadata\nafter writing sysfs. Without bioset, this triggers a NULL pointer\ndereference as below:\n\n BUG: kernel NULL pointer dereference, address: 0000000000000020\n Call Trace:\n  md_update_sb+0x658/0xe00\n  new_level_store+0xc5/0x120\n  md_attr_store+0xc9/0x1e0\n  sysfs_kf_write+0x6f/0xa0\n  kernfs_fop_write_iter+0x141/0x2a0\n  vfs_write+0x1fc/0x5a0\n  ksys_write+0x79/0x180\n  __x64_sys_write+0x1d/0x30\n  x64_sys_call+0x2818/0x2880\n  do_syscall_64+0xa9/0x580\n  entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x4b/0x53\n\nReproducer\n```\n  mdadm -CR /dev/md0 -l1 -n2 /dev/sd[cd]\n  echo inactive \u003e /sys/block/md0/md/array_state\n  echo 10 \u003e /sys/block/md0/md/new_level\n```\n\nmddev_init() can only be called once per mddev, no need to test if bioset\nhas been initialized anymore."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68368",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-68368\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2025-68368](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68368)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmd: init bioset in mddev_init\n\nIO operations may be needed before md_run(), such as updating metadata\nafter writing sysfs. Without bioset, this triggers a NULL pointer\ndereference as below:\n\n BUG: kernel NULL pointer dereference, address: 0000000000000020\n Call Trace:\n  md_update_sb+0x658/0xe00\n  new_level_store+0xc5/0x120\n  md_attr_store+0xc9/0x1e0\n  sysfs_kf_write+0x6f/0xa0\n  kernfs_fop_write_iter+0x141/0x2a0\n  vfs_write+0x1fc/0x5a0\n  ksys_write+0x79/0x180\n  __x64_sys_write+0x1d/0x30\n  x64_sys_call+0x2818/0x2880\n  do_syscall_64+0xa9/0x580\n  entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x4b/0x53\n\nReproducer\n```\n  mdadm -CR /dev/md0 -l1 -n2 /dev/sd[cd]\n  echo inactive \u003e /sys/block/md0/md/array_state\n  echo 10 \u003e /sys/block/md0/md/new_level\n```\n\nmddev_init() can only be called once per mddev, no need to test if bioset\nhas been initialized anymore.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-68368**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2025-68368](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68368)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmd: init bioset in mddev_init\n\nIO operations may be needed before md_run(), such as updating metadata\nafter writing sysfs. Without bioset, this triggers a NULL pointer\ndereference as below:\n\n BUG: kernel NULL pointer dereference, address: 0000000000000020\n Call Trace:\n  md_update_sb+0x658/0xe00\n  new_level_store+0xc5/0x120\n  md_attr_store+0xc9/0x1e0\n  sysfs_kf_write+0x6f/0xa0\n  kernfs_fop_write_iter+0x141/0x2a0\n  vfs_write+0x1fc/0x5a0\n  ksys_write+0x79/0x180\n  __x64_sys_write+0x1d/0x30\n  x64_sys_call+0x2818/0x2880\n  do_syscall_64+0xa9/0x580\n  entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x4b/0x53\n\nReproducer\n```\n  mdadm -CR /dev/md0 -l1 -n2 /dev/sd[cd]\n  echo inactive \u003e /sys/block/md0/md/array_state\n  echo 10 \u003e /sys/block/md0/md/new_level\n```\n\nmddev_init() can only be called once per mddev, no need to test if bioset\nhas been initialized anymore."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-68376",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: coresight: ETR: Fix ETR buffer use-after-free issue"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncoresight: ETR: Fix ETR buffer use-after-free issue\n\nWhen ETR is enabled as CS_MODE_SYSFS, if the buffer size is changed\nand enabled again, currently sysfs_buf will point to the newly\nallocated memory(buf_new) and free the old memory(buf_old). But the\netr_buf that is being used by the ETR remains pointed to buf_old, not\nupdated to buf_new. In this case, it will result in a memory\nuse-after-free issue.\n\nFix this by checking ETR's mode before updating and releasing buf_old,\nif the mode is CS_MODE_SYSFS, then skip updating and releasing it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68376",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-68376\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2025-68376](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68376)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncoresight: ETR: Fix ETR buffer use-after-free issue\n\nWhen ETR is enabled as CS_MODE_SYSFS, if the buffer size is changed\nand enabled again, currently sysfs_buf will point to the newly\nallocated memory(buf_new) and free the old memory(buf_old). But the\netr_buf that is being used by the ETR remains pointed to buf_old, not\nupdated to buf_new. In this case, it will result in a memory\nuse-after-free issue.\n\nFix this by checking ETR's mode before updating and releasing buf_old,\nif the mode is CS_MODE_SYSFS, then skip updating and releasing it.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-68376**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2025-68376](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68376)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncoresight: ETR: Fix ETR buffer use-after-free issue\n\nWhen ETR is enabled as CS_MODE_SYSFS, if the buffer size is changed\nand enabled again, currently sysfs_buf will point to the newly\nallocated memory(buf_new) and free the old memory(buf_old). But the\netr_buf that is being used by the ETR remains pointed to buf_old, not\nupdated to buf_new. In this case, it will result in a memory\nuse-after-free issue.\n\nFix this by checking ETR's mode before updating and releasing buf_old,\nif the mode is CS_MODE_SYSFS, then skip updating and releasing it."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-68729",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: wifi: ath12k: Fix MSDU buffer types handling in RX error path"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: ath12k: Fix MSDU buffer types handling in RX error path\n\nCurrently, packets received on the REO exception ring from\nunassociated peers are of MSDU buffer type, while the driver expects\nlink descriptor type packets. These packets are not parsed further due\nto a return check on packet type in ath12k_hal_desc_reo_parse_err(),\nbut the associated skb is not freed. This may lead to kernel\ncrashes and buffer leaks.\n\nHence to fix, update the RX error handler to explicitly drop\nMSDU buffer type packets received on the REO exception ring.\nThis prevents further processing of invalid packets and ensures\nstability in the RX error handling path.\n\nTested-on: QCN9274 hw2.0 PCI WLAN.WBE.1.4.1-00199-QCAHKSWPL_SILICONZ-1"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68729",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-68729\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2025-68729](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68729)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: ath12k: Fix MSDU buffer types handling in RX error path\n\nCurrently, packets received on the REO exception ring from\nunassociated peers are of MSDU buffer type, while the driver expects\nlink descriptor type packets. These packets are not parsed further due\nto a return check on packet type in ath12k_hal_desc_reo_parse_err(),\nbut the associated skb is not freed. This may lead to kernel\ncrashes and buffer leaks.\n\nHence to fix, update the RX error handler to explicitly drop\nMSDU buffer type packets received on the REO exception ring.\nThis prevents further processing of invalid packets and ensures\nstability in the RX error handling path.\n\nTested-on: QCN9274 hw2.0 PCI WLAN.WBE.1.4.1-00199-QCAHKSWPL_SILICONZ-1",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-68729**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2025-68729](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68729)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: ath12k: Fix MSDU buffer types handling in RX error path\n\nCurrently, packets received on the REO exception ring from\nunassociated peers are of MSDU buffer type, while the driver expects\nlink descriptor type packets. These packets are not parsed further due\nto a return check on packet type in ath12k_hal_desc_reo_parse_err(),\nbut the associated skb is not freed. This may lead to kernel\ncrashes and buffer leaks.\n\nHence to fix, update the RX error handler to explicitly drop\nMSDU buffer type packets received on the REO exception ring.\nThis prevents further processing of invalid packets and ensures\nstability in the RX error handling path.\n\nTested-on: QCN9274 hw2.0 PCI WLAN.WBE.1.4.1-00199-QCAHKSWPL_SILICONZ-1"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
