            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-74451",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: drm/panthor: validate firmware interface structure sizes"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ndrm/panthor: validate firmware interface structure sizes\n\niface_fw_to_cpu_addr() only checks that the firmware-provided MCU virtual\naddress points inside the shared section. The returned pointer is later\nused as a full firmware interface structure, so accepting an address near\nthe end of the shared section can still lead to out-of-bounds accesses.\n\nPass the expected object size to iface_fw_to_cpu_addr() and reject ranges\nthat do not fit entirely in the shared section."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74451",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-74451\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.12.105-1\nLink: [CVE-2026-74451](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74451)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ndrm/panthor: validate firmware interface structure sizes\n\niface_fw_to_cpu_addr() only checks that the firmware-provided MCU virtual\naddress points inside the shared section. The returned pointer is later\nused as a full firmware interface structure, so accepting an address near\nthe end of the shared section can still lead to out-of-bounds accesses.\n\nPass the expected object size to iface_fw_to_cpu_addr() and reject ranges\nthat do not fit entirely in the shared section.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-74451**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.12.105-1|[CVE-2026-74451](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74451)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ndrm/panthor: validate firmware interface structure sizes\n\niface_fw_to_cpu_addr() only checks that the firmware-provided MCU virtual\naddress points inside the shared section. The returned pointer is later\nused as a full firmware interface structure, so accepting an address near\nthe end of the shared section can still lead to out-of-bounds accesses.\n\nPass the expected object size to iface_fw_to_cpu_addr() and reject ranges\nthat do not fit entirely in the shared section."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-74452",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: drm/panthor: reject firmware sections with oversized data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ndrm/panthor: reject firmware sections with oversized data\n\nIn panthor_fw_load_section_entry(), the data size to copy is calculated\nwithout validating it against the allocated section_size:\n\n    section-\u003edata.size = hdr.data.end - hdr.data.start;\n\nIf a crafted firmware sets data.size larger than the allocated memory,\nthis could cause a heap buffer overflow in panthor_fw_init_section_mem()\n\n    memcpy(section-\u003emem-\u003ekmap, section-\u003edata.buf, section-\u003edata.size);\n\nAdditionally, if the section-\u003edata.size exceeds the BO size, could this\nmemset underflow the size calculation, leading to a massive out-of-bounds\nzeroing of kernel memory?\n\n    memset(section-\u003emem-\u003ekmap + section-\u003edata.size, 0,\n           panthor_kernel_bo_size(section-\u003emem) - section-\u003edata.size);\n\nReject section entries whose initial data is larger than the section size."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74452",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-74452\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.12.105-1\nLink: [CVE-2026-74452](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74452)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ndrm/panthor: reject firmware sections with oversized data\n\nIn panthor_fw_load_section_entry(), the data size to copy is calculated\nwithout validating it against the allocated section_size:\n\n    section-\u003edata.size = hdr.data.end - hdr.data.start;\n\nIf a crafted firmware sets data.size larger than the allocated memory,\nthis could cause a heap buffer overflow in panthor_fw_init_section_mem()\n\n    memcpy(section-\u003emem-\u003ekmap, section-\u003edata.buf, section-\u003edata.size);\n\nAdditionally, if the section-\u003edata.size exceeds the BO size, could this\nmemset underflow the size calculation, leading to a massive out-of-bounds\nzeroing of kernel memory?\n\n    memset(section-\u003emem-\u003ekmap + section-\u003edata.size, 0,\n           panthor_kernel_bo_size(section-\u003emem) - section-\u003edata.size);\n\nReject section entries whose initial data is larger than the section size.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-74452**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev|6.12.105-1|[CVE-2026-74452](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74452)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ndrm/panthor: reject firmware sections with oversized data\n\nIn panthor_fw_load_section_entry(), the data size to copy is calculated\nwithout validating it against the allocated section_size:\n\n    section-\u003edata.size = hdr.data.end - hdr.data.start;\n\nIf a crafted firmware sets data.size larger than the allocated memory,\nthis could cause a heap buffer overflow in panthor_fw_init_section_mem()\n\n    memcpy(section-\u003emem-\u003ekmap, section-\u003edata.buf, section-\u003edata.size);\n\nAdditionally, if the section-\u003edata.size exceeds the BO size, could this\nmemset underflow the size calculation, leading to a massive out-of-bounds\nzeroing of kernel memory?\n\n    memset(section-\u003emem-\u003ekmap + section-\u003edata.size, 0,\n           panthor_kernel_bo_size(section-\u003emem) - section-\u003edata.size);\n\nReject section entries whose initial data is larger than the section size."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
