            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-80574",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:  I ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nInput: focaltech - fix array out-of-bounds in focaltech_process_rel_packet\n\nMake finger2 (and also finger1) unsigned, so that if the finger index in\nthe packet is 0 then subtracting 1 creates an array index which overflows\nabove the existing check for FOC_MAX_FINGERS, as the existing comment says\nit should, instead of writing to state-\u003efingers[-1]."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80574",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-80574\nSeverity: UNKNOWN\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.12.105-1\nLink: [CVE-2026-80574](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80574)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nInput: focaltech - fix array out-of-bounds in focaltech_process_rel_packet\n\nMake finger2 (and also finger1) unsigned, so that if the finger index in\nthe packet is 0 then subtracting 1 creates an array index which overflows\nabove the existing check for FOC_MAX_FINGERS, as the existing comment says\nit should, instead of writing to state-\u003efingers[-1].",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-80574**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|UNKNOWN|linux-libc-dev|6.12.105-1|[CVE-2026-80574](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80574)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nInput: focaltech - fix array out-of-bounds in focaltech_process_rel_packet\n\nMake finger2 (and also finger1) unsigned, so that if the finger index in\nthe packet is 0 then subtracting 1 creates an array index which overflows\nabove the existing check for FOC_MAX_FINGERS, as the existing comment says\nit should, instead of writing to state-\u003efingers[-1]."
              },
