            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-80707",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:  c ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncan: j1939: transport: j1939_session_fresh_new(): initialize receive buffer\n\nZero the allocated buffer in j1939_session_fresh_new() to ensure it\ncontains no residual data.\n\nWhile there is a potential performance impact if users allocate maximum\nsized ETP buffers, most real-world use cases are not noticeably affected\nsince the maximum known buffer size is typically around 65K.\n\n[mkl: add Message-ID]"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80707",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-80707\nSeverity: UNKNOWN\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: 6.12.105-1\nLink: [CVE-2026-80707](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80707)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncan: j1939: transport: j1939_session_fresh_new(): initialize receive buffer\n\nZero the allocated buffer in j1939_session_fresh_new() to ensure it\ncontains no residual data.\n\nWhile there is a potential performance impact if users allocate maximum\nsized ETP buffers, most real-world use cases are not noticeably affected\nsince the maximum known buffer size is typically around 65K.\n\n[mkl: add Message-ID]",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-80707**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|UNKNOWN|linux-libc-dev|6.12.105-1|[CVE-2026-80707](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80707)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncan: j1939: transport: j1939_session_fresh_new(): initialize receive buffer\n\nZero the allocated buffer in j1939_session_fresh_new() to ensure it\ncontains no residual data.\n\nWhile there is a potential performance impact if users allocate maximum\nsized ETP buffers, most real-world use cases are not noticeably affected\nsince the maximum known buffer size is typically around 65K.\n\n[mkl: add Message-ID]"
              },
